<p class='text'>In  <a href='./why-estimate'>another article</a> I went into great detail about how it might be possible to obtain accurate estimates if a client wants them, and how incredibly difficult and expensive it is.  Reading that article back today (in 2025; the article was written in 2008), I almost wonder if I was being satirical.  However, while I may have been <i>frustrated</i>, I was endeavouring to be serious.  And, in particular, I was keen to make two <i>huge</i> points:</p>
<p class='text'>* It's really difficult to estimate "something" without knowing exactly what that "something" is;* The number of dimensions (knowable and unknowable) is so large that even obtaining a handle on them is almost impossible.</p>
<p class='text'>In this article, I want to come at the whole question of estimation from a different (possibly more truculent) perspective: tell me why you want this estimate and what you're going to do with it and I'll give you some, limited, information.</p>
<p class='text'>There are three main things that developers think clients want estimates for:</p>
<p class='text'>* To hold against them later when the client is upset with what has been delivered;* To fill in pretty charts and diagrams for them to show to investors;* To plan out marketing and sales campaigns.</p>
<p class='text'>While I think all of these things happen, I think only the last one is genuinely valid.  But having worked closely with clients over the years and <i>asked</i> them why they want an estimate, most commonly it is quite simple: <i>to make decisions</i>.  Given any feature <tt>X</tt>, the client knows that they "want" it, but they also want features <tt>Y</tt> and <tt>Z</tt> (and, usually, 2000 others).  But they know enough to know they are resource-limited: so which of these stories should they ask for?</p>
<p class='text'>They probably haven't calculated the dollar value of any of these features, but they probably have an instinct about the relative <i>value</i> of the three.  And what they want from developers is a similar instinct about the relative <i>cost</i> of the three.  And, in line with many developers before me, as long as we can agree on what <tt>X</tt> <i>is</i>, I think it's fairly easy to put any story into one of three categories: "that can be done within a day or so", "that will probably fit in a (1-week) iteration" or "that would be a multi-iteration story that we need to break down somehow".</p>
<p class='text'>And then it is possible to have a discussion between client and developers about what value can be extracted from what work and thus, finally, which parts of which stories to include in <i>this</i> iteration, which is all that matters.</p>
